 Monday ✅. Today we track the fallout from the bombshell admission by Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang this weekend. 

 Today's newsletter is 873 words, a 3½-minute read. 

 📈 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed up 2.4%.

 🔥 Today's stock spotlight: Skydance (+8.6%) said it's planning at least $6 billion in cost cuts after its deal with Warner Bros.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: No Plan B 
 
 By Nathan Bomey and Emily Peck 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang. Photo: Jose Sarmento Matos/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Nvidia is coming under fire for having no clear succession plan to replace Jensen Huang in the event that something unexpected happens — like an international incident with him at the center .

 Catch up quick: Huang — who founded the chip giant, serves as its all-powerful CEO and exerts significant influence over the global AI economy — admitted to having an affair with a woman suspected of being a Chinese spy.

 Nvidia's board announced an independent investigation yesterday, and experts say Huang's chances of keeping his job are a coin flip . The big picture: The company, by all accounts, has no clear bench of potential replacements.

 Huang famously has dozens of direct reports, ensuring that no single individual — other than him — has a wide-ranging purview at the company. 🔍 State of play: The highest-profile member of Huang's executive team is Colette Kress, Nvidia's CFO, who appears alongside him during the company's earnings calls.

 But analysts say Kress — who has spent her career in financial roles — isn't necessarily a natural successor to Huang in his role as an innovator and visionary for the AI age. 🗣️ What they're saying: "It's ridiculous that they have nobody in management identified," says Jeffrey Sonnenfeld, a professor at the Yale School of Management who is one of the nation's premier CEO whisperers .

 For now, Nvidia investors are anxiously awaiting results of the probe — but in general, most say they are hoping he's able to stay.

 "In some ways, he's irreplaceable," says Michael Monaghan, partner at Founder ETFs, an Nvidia investor. Nvidia had no immediate comment today.

 📉 The impact: The company's shares closed down 4.3%.

