 2. Should he stay, or should he go 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Gabriella Turrisi/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 All eyes now are on what Nvidia's board decides to do, Nathan and Emily write .

 The affair itself probably isn't a fireable offense, since it wasn't with an employee, business ethics experts say. But if Huang inadvertently exposed secrets to an American adversary, that could get him booted. 🕵 The big picture: Directors of the second most valuable company in the world are being forced to confront the possibility that Huang's behavior could have compromised the AI chip giant's secrets — and the nation's security.

 🗣️ What we're watching: "Given the seriousness of the allegations, I do wonder why he wasn't put on some sort of administrative leave," says Charles Elson, a corporate governance expert and former University of Delaware professor.

 The distraction factor is also notable here, as the board has launched what it says is an independent investigation that could consume much of Huang's attention, and Huang has already been called to a closed-door meeting with Republican lawmakers . "He's gonna be really busy answering questions instead of running the business," says Yale's Sonnenfeld. Reality check: Support from investors, President Trump and other AI leaders will be a powerful force that traders believe will keep Huang on the job.

 On Kalshi , traders say the chances of Huang leaving the job before Jan. 1, 2029, are 33%, after briefly jumping above 55% earlier today. Go deeper 

 
 
